     That doesn't sound easier to me, and it clobbers your score
file.  You just need to navigate two directory levels deeper and
delete one file whose name will vary depending upon dungeon level.
The data is kept in NetHack.app/nethackdir/, and bones files are
named bon*.* there.

     As has already been mentioned, if you're going to delete any
"bad" bones files then you should also be deleting "good" ones to
balance things out.  (Reinstalling certainly achieves that, but
still seems awfully drastic.)  And instead of deleting anything,
you can always switch to explore mode or even quit when you arrive
at the dangerous level, provided you recognize it prior to being
killed.  As soon as you enter the level and it gets loaded from a
bones file instead of being created from scratch, that bones file
will be gone and can't be replaced by a new one if you don't die
there again.  Running away--or forward--while staying in normal
play is less cheesy and has the same effect if you manage to get
off that level; if/when you die somewhere else, all data on any
other levels will be gone for good.

     Lastly, even if you get killed on that level repeatedly, the
chance for saving a bones file is random--it's more likely as you
get deeper into the dungeon--so eventually one of your deaths will
fail to save bones and then the next game to reach there will have
to use an ordinary level for it.  If you haven't looked into the
data directory after the subsequent death to check, your bones
file might even already be gone, especially since it was a shallow
one....


